    excerpt: "What window installation leads actually cost in 2026, how exclusivity and project size affect pricing, and how to judge whether a quoted rate makes financial sense for your business.",
    metaDescription: "What window installation leads cost in 2026 — exclusive vs shared pricing, how project size affects rates, and how to evaluate a fair cost per lead.",
    category: "Home Services Marketing",
    readTime: "6 min read",
    publishedDate: "2026-09-10",
    updatedDate: "2026-08-05",
    content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Window installation leads sit toward the higher end of home services pricing, and for good reason: the average job value is larger than most repair trades, and a homeowner who submits a genuine request is usually a serious buyer rather than someone casually curious about pricing. Understanding what actually drives window installation leads cost up or down helps a company judge whether a quoted price from a provider is reasonable for the market and project type in question." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Typical Price Ranges in 2026" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Shared window installation leads generally run $25 to $60 per lead, while exclusive window installation leads, sold to a single company only, typically run $50 to $150 depending on project scope and region. Full-home replacement leads, where a homeowner is requesting quotes for eight or more windows at once, command a premium over leads for a single window repair or replacement, since the total project value is substantially higher and closing that lead is worth considerably more to the business." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Why Project Size Changes the Math" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A lead for a single cracked window glass replacement and a lead for a full-home window and door installation project shouldn't be priced the same, even though both technically fall under \"window leads.\" A well-run lead provider filters by project scope so a company isn't paying premium pricing for a small repair job, or getting an underpriced lead for a large full-home project that deserved a higher-touch, better-qualified match. Asking a provider directly how they segment pricing by project size is a reasonable question before committing to volume." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Exclusive vs. Shared for This Trade Specifically" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Exclusivity matters more for window installation than for some faster-moving trades, precisely because the sales cycle is longer. A shared lead being contacted by three companies at once often results in the homeowner simply going with whoever shows up first for an in-home estimate, which can mean losing jobs on price or timing rather than on the actual quality of work offered. Exclusive window installation leads cost more per lead but tend to produce a noticeably better return once you factor in the higher average project value at stake." },
      { type: "heading", text: "What Affects Price Beyond Exclusivity" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Project size — full-home replacement leads cost more than single-window repair or replacement leads.",
        "Specialty service — tinting, shutter, screen, and glass repair leads are typically priced differently than standard installation leads.",
        "Geography — leads in higher-cost-of-living metro markets generally command higher prices than smaller or rural markets.",
        "Season — leads generated during peak fall and spring demand windows often cost more than off-season leads.",
      ]},
      { type: "heading", text: "How Eilite Approaches Window Lead Pricing" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Eilite's [window installation lead generation program](/solutions/windows-installation-lead-generation) filters leads by project scope, material preference, and location before delivery, so companies are paying for leads that actually match their crew's specialty rather than a generic, unsorted list. Pay-per-call options are also available for companies that want to speak with a homeowner directly rather than working from a submitted form, which tends to compress the sales cycle for buyers who are already comparing quotes and ready to schedule an estimate." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Calculating Whether the Price Is Actually Fair" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "As with any trade, the per-lead price is only half the picture. A company paying $90 for an exclusive full-home replacement lead that closes at a $6,000 average project value, with a 25% close rate, is spending roughly $360 per signed job against $6,000 in revenue, an excellent return by almost any standard. The same $90 lead closing at only 5% is a much weaker outcome, and that gap is usually a sign of a slow estimate scheduling process or a weak in-home sales presentation rather than a genuinely bad lead source." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Testing Before Committing to Volume" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Because window leads run more expensive than smaller repair-trade leads, it's especially worth testing a modest batch first, ideally at least ten to fifteen leads, tracking appointment show rate, close rate, and average project value before scaling monthly spend. A short trial like this reveals within a few weeks whether a provider's leads genuinely match what a company can profitably convert." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "How much do window installation leads typically cost?", a: "Shared leads generally run $25 to $60, while exclusive leads run $50 to $150, with full-home replacement leads priced at the higher end of that range due to their larger average project value." },
      { q: "Why do exclusive window leads cost so much more than shared ones?", a: "Window installation involves a longer sales cycle with an in-home estimate, and exclusivity avoids losing the job to whichever competitor happens to show up first, which tends to justify the higher price through a better close rate." },
      { q: "Do specialty services like tinting or shutter repair cost the same as standard installation leads?", a: "Not usually — pricing for specialty services is typically set separately from standard installation leads, since project value and demand differ meaningfully between these niches." },
      { q: "Is it worth testing a small batch of leads before committing to a monthly budget?", a: "Yes, testing ten to fifteen leads first and tracking show rate, close rate, and average project value is the most reliable way to confirm a provider's leads fit your business before scaling spend." },
      { q: "Does season affect window installation lead pricing?", a: "Yes — pricing tends to rise during peak fall and spring demand windows when homeowner search volume and competitive bidding both increase." },
    ],
